+// Device extensions that MUST NOT be advertised to the guest — DERIVED, not hand-curated.
+//
+// Each name in the generated list below owns at least one VkStructureType that this build's cereal
+// codegen lists in goldfish_vk_extension_struct_size (524 types) but does NOT handle in
+// reservedunmarshal_extension_struct (195 types). A guest that enables the extension and chains the
+// struct hits reservedunmarshal's default case and abort()s the whole VMM. Filtering the extension
+// out of vkEnumerateDeviceExtensionProperties means the guest never enables it, so the struct is
+// never put on the wire. No wire-format guessing is involved — see the .inc header for why
+// softening the abort() into a skip is strictly worse than aborting.
+//
+// POLARITY IS INVERTED ON PURPOSE. This used to be a hand-written nine-name array, which was a
+// subset of a 166-extension problem: the same analysis that predicted VK_EXT_memory_budget (the
+// empirically confirmed member — it was aborting the VMM) predicts 165 more, and
+// VK_EXT_extended_dynamic_state3 is one of them, on a host that advertises it and a guest ANGLE
+// that enables it when present. So the strip list is now GENERATED from the pinned tree's own
+// codegen and the exceptions are enumerated instead, each with a reason. Adding a host extension
+// can no longer silently add an abort; it can only add an entry to a list somebody has to justify.
+//
+// Regenerate with `aemu-cereal-gap generate`; ci/hvf-doctor.sh runs `aemu-cereal-gap verify` so a
+// gfxstream pin bump that moves the codegen fails at doctor time, not at abort() time mid-boot.
+//
+// This is deliberately NOT #if defined(__APPLE__): the codegen gap is a property of this host
+// build, not of macOS.
+#include "VkDecoderCerealGap.inc"
+
+// Extensions that are in the generated gap but are advertised to the guest ANYWAY.
+//
+// SHRUNK TO ONE ENTRY. This list originally held ten names, each a bet that the guest would never
+// chain a struct the host could size but not decode. Nine of those bets no longer need making: the
+// cereal codegen now emits real marshal/unmarshal/deepcopy/transform cases for them, so they are
+// not in aemu_cereal_gap::kCerealGapExtensions at all and cerealGapHidesExtension() could never
+// have returned true for them. Removing them was therefore provably a no-op, not a capability
+// change — which is why it was safe to do in bulk despite the standing rule below.
+//
+// The nine that graduated (now decoded, not merely tolerated):
+//   VK_EXT_buffer_device_address, VK_EXT_depth_clip_control, VK_EXT_image_2d_view_of_3d,
+//   VK_EXT_pipeline_robustness, VK_EXT_sample_locations, VK_EXT_shader_atomic_float,
+//   VK_KHR_present_id, VK_KHR_present_wait, VK_KHR_shader_subgroup_uniform_control_flow
+//
+// SHRINKING THIS LIST FURTHER IS A PER-ENTRY, BOOT-VERIFIED EXERCISE. Do not bulk-delete the
+// remainder: removing the last entry IS a capability change, and the only evidence that matters is
+// a boot with it gone. Conversely, do not ADD to it without an equally concrete reason — an
+// addition re-arms an abort().
+static constexpr const char* const kCerealGapExtensionsKeptAnyway[] = {
+    // Pushing descriptors inline instead of allocating a descriptor set.
+    //
+    // NOW A PROVABLE NO-OP, kept as the array's required single element and as a marker.
+    //
+    // It used to be load-bearing: the extension's only pNext struct was
+    // VK_STRUCTURE_TYPE_PHYSICAL_DEVICE_PUSH_DESCRIPTOR_PROPERTIES_KHR, which the 1.3.269-era
+    // registry knew only under the KHR spelling, owned by a feature the codegen would not decode.
+    // The v1.4.348 registry bump (Phase C) promoted push descriptors into core Vulkan 1.4, and
+    // VK_VERSION_1_4 IS in the codegen's SUPPORTED_FEATURES — so the struct is decoded under its
+    // core spelling, VK_KHR_push_descriptor is no longer in aemu_cereal_gap::kCerealGapExtensions
+    // at all, and cerealGapHidesExtension() can no longer reach this loop for it.
+    //
+    // WHAT DID NOT CHANGE, and is the reason this comment is not simply deleted:
+    // vkCmdPushDescriptorSetWithTemplateKHR still passes an opaque `const void* pData` whose layout
+    // is described by a VkDescriptorUpdateTemplate rather than by the registry, and the cereal
+    // codegen still cannot marshal it. The host has no OP_vkCmdPushDescriptorSetWithTemplateKHR;
+    // the prebuilt guest ICD does (20221). If a guest ever calls it, that opcode lands on
+    // VkDecoder's `default:` arm, which returns early and DESYNCHRONISES the stream.
+    //
+    // Why that is still dormant, stated as a mechanism rather than a hope: the guest ICD's own
+    // hardcoded allow-list (ResourceTracker.cpp, allowedExtensionNames) does NOT contain
+    // VK_KHR_push_descriptor — verified after the Phase B widening (89 -> 115 device extensions) —
+    // so the guest filters the name out before Android sees it and never enables it. An extension
+    // that is never enabled cannot put its commands on the wire. IF THAT GUEST ALLOW-LIST EVER
+    // GAINS VK_KHR_push_descriptor, the template entry point must be given a decoder FIRST.
+    "VK_KHR_push_descriptor",
+};
+// True when `name` must be hidden from the guest.
+//
+// TWO independent hazards, and they need opposite logic:
+//
+//  1. BLACKLIST — the cereal gap. The codegen can SIZE the extension's pNext structs but cannot
+//     DECODE them, so reservedunmarshal_extension_struct()'s default: arm abort()s the VMM. These
+//     names are known ahead of time, hence a strip list.
+//
+//  2. WHITELIST — the registry horizon. The host driver is a live Mesa build that moves
+//     independently of the pinned Vulkan registry the cereal was generated from. An extension the
+//     driver advertises that the registry has never heard of has its VkStructureTypes in NEITHER
+//     switch, so it aborts one step EARLIER, inside goldfish_vk_extension_struct_size(). This
+//     cannot be a strip list: the dangerous names are by definition unknown when the list is built.
+//     KosmicKrisp used to advertise 26 such extensions (VK_KHR_maintenance6..10, VK_KHR_robustness2,
+//     VK_KHR_vertex_attribute_divisor, ...) and the guest ICD's own allow-list permitted several of
+//     them — a live abort path the gap list is structurally blind to. Phase C closed that instance
+//     of it by moving the vendored registry from Vulkan-Docs v1.3.269 to v1.4.348 (552 -> 682
+//     extension names), which is why the count is currently ZERO. It is not permanently zero: Mesa
+//     keeps moving, so this whitelist stays, and the count is a measurement, not an invariant.
+//
+// The allowlist can only override (1). Overriding (2) would be advertising an extension whose
+// structs the host cannot even measure, which is never defensible.
+static bool cerealGapHidesExtension(const char* name) {
+    if (!aemu_cereal_gap::CerealRegistryKnowsExtension(name)) return true;
+    for (const char* kept : kCerealGapExtensionsKeptAnyway) {
+        if (strcmp(name, kept) == 0) return false;
+    }
+    for (const char* hidden : aemu_cereal_gap::kCerealGapExtensions) {
+        if (strcmp(name, hidden) == 0) return true;
+    }
+    return false;
+}
